b0c290ff3.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the angle $\\overset{^}{COD}$ (or $\\overset{^}{DOC}$) has a measure of ${30}^{∘}.$" 13194139534648 17592186045417 true] [17592186134170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534648 17592186045417 true] [17592186134170 1071 "![](images/f2/f2df084837126729c944d54bd9cc9667132c44bdd66e8eb27a148cebe2192386.png)\n\nWhich angle has a measure of $38^{∘}$?" 13194139534648 17592186045417 false] [17592186134170 1071 "![](images/f2/f2df084837126729c944d54bd9cc9667132c44bdd66e8eb27a148cebe2192386.png)\n\nWhich angle has a measure of ${38}^{∘}?$" 13194139534648 17592186045417 true] [17592186134170 1074 "Let's highlight the angle with a measure ${38}^{∘}$ on our diagram.\n\n![](images/aa/aa5966ce20b4ced2830fea17421079275d5554ad158e857570bed564acbd252f.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the angle $∠AOB$ (or $∠BOA$) has a measure of ${38}^{∘}.$" 13194139534648 17592186045417 true] [17592186134171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534648 17592186045417 true] [17592186134171 1071 "![](images/80/800d552240f4387fcd5efd57bee5f7841bfe262b4465bab3f9b224e2909d64c5.png)\n\nWhich numbered angle refers to the same angle as $∠MNB$?" 13194139534648 17592186045417 false] [17592186134171 1071 "![](images/80/800d552240f4387fcd5efd57bee5f7841bfe262b4465bab3f9b224e2909d64c5.png)\n\nWhich numbered angle refers to the same angle as $∠MNB?$" 13194139534648 17592186045417 true] [17592186134171 1074 "If we trace along the letters of the angle name, from $M$ to $N$ to $B$, we see that $∠MNB$ refers to the angle $∠4.$\n\n![](images/f7/f739595dbf25ff18b2fea0085fb83ae741ff3f90414ac8603f87b6b7f51314b2.png)\n\nTherefore, $∠4$ is another name for $∠MNB.$" 13194139534648 17592186045417 true] [17592186135018 1064 "An **angle** is formed whenever two rays meet at a point.\n\nThe common point shared by the rays is called the **vertex**, and the rays themselves are called the **sides** of the angle.\n\n![](images/ca/caa6fe6d949902d06c155f109fc2202bc4adbfc0c324591100a56337f6841279.png)\n\nConsider the following angle, made up of the rays $\\overrightarrow{{\\color{blue}{A}}B}$ and $\\overrightarrow{{\\color{blue}{A}}C}$.\n\n![](images/d4/d4a10fda5cbf508754812bb114e80de3f3aad529c54d6b82538284bda58cd737.png)\n\nThere are several different ways of labeling this angle.\n\n- One way to write this angle is as follows:\n$\\angle C\\!{\\color{blue}{A}}B$\nThe symbol $\\angle$ means \"angle.\" Notice that the vertex ${\\color{blue}{A}}$ is in the middle.\n- We can also swap the order in which we write the endpoints. For example,\n$\\angle B{\\color{blue}{A}}C$.\nNotice that the vertex ${\\color{blue}{A}}$ is still in the middle.\n- To save space, we sometimes write $\\angle {\\color{blue}{A}}$.\n- Finally, instead of using the $\\angle$ symbol, we can write $\\widehat{C{\\color{blue}{A}}B}$ or $\\widehat{B{\\color{blue}{A}}C}$." 13194139534648 17592186045417 false] [17592186135018 1064 "An **angle** is formed whenever two rays meet at a point. \n\nThe common point shared by the rays is called the **vertex**, and the rays themselves are called the **sides** of the angle.\n\n![](images/ca/caa6fe6d949902d06c155f109fc2202bc4adbfc0c324591100a56337f6841279.png)\n\nConsider the following angle, made up of the rays $\\overrightarrow{{\\color{blue}{A}}B}$ and $\\overrightarrow{{\\color{blue}{A}}C}.$\n\n![](images/d4/d4a10fda5cbf508754812bb114e80de3f3aad529c54d6b82538284bda58cd737.png)\n\nThere are several different ways of labeling this angle.\n\n- One way to write this angle is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\angle C\\!{\\color{blue}{A}}B\n$$\n\n \nThe symbol $\\angle$ means \"angle.\" Notice that the vertex ${\\color{blue}{A}}$ is in the middle.\n\n- We can also swap the order in which we write the endpoints. For example,\n\n$$\n\\angle B{\\color{blue}{A}}C.\n$$\n\n \nNotice that the vertex ${\\color{blue}{A}}$ is still in the middle.\n\n- To save space, we sometimes write $\\angle {\\color{blue}{A}}.$\n\n- Finally, instead of using the $\\angle$ symbol, we can write $\\widehat{C{\\color{blue}{A}}B}$ or $\\widehat{B{\\color{blue}{A}}C}.$" 13194139534648 17592186045417 true] [17592186135150 1064 "The **measure** of an angle is the amount of \"turn\" from one side of the angle to the other.\n\nWe measure angles in **degrees**, which have the symbol $^\\circ$.\n\nLet's look at a few examples of angles with their measures.\n\n![](images/7c/7c8a1fb7268052f44c88734607cd8e38ae8b57b924ec9fb65a6cb8918c60266f.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The more \"turn\" there is, the larger the angle's measure.\n- A corner angle (like the angle $C$ above) has a measure of $90^\\circ$. An angle with measure $90^\\circ$ is called a **right angle.**\n\nWe use the letter $m$ to represent the measure of the angle. For example,\n\n$$\nm \\angle C = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe'd read this as \"the measure of the angle $C$ is $90$ degrees.\"" 13194139534648 17592186045417 false] [17592186135150 1064 "The **measure** of an angle is the amount of \"turn\" from one side of the angle to the other. \n\nWe measure angles in **degrees**, which have the symbol $^\\circ.$ \n\nLet's look at a few examples of angles with their measures.\n\n![](images/7c/7c8a1fb7268052f44c88734607cd8e38ae8b57b924ec9fb65a6cb8918c60266f.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The more \"turn\" there is, the larger the angle's measure.\n\n- A corner angle (like the angle $C$ above) has a measure of $90^\\circ.$ An angle with measure $90^\\circ$ is called a **right angle.**\n\nWe use the letter $m$ to represent the measure of the angle. For example,\n\n$$\nm \\angle C = 90^\\circ.\n$$\n\nWe'd read this as \"the measure of the angle $C$ is $90$ degrees.\"" 13194139534648 17592186045417 true] [17592186430830 1080 "{115}^{∘}" 13194139534648 17592186045417 true] [17592186430830 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534648 17592186045417 true] [17592186430830 1083 #uuid "046d6e93-e0be-5de4-af54-a408b6001a38" 13194139534648 17592186045417 true] [17592186430831 1080 "{78}^{∘}" 13194139534648 1759